### Step 1: Find the Known Crib (The Guaranteed Plaintext)

  In picoCTF, every flag starts with the format picoCTF{...}.
  Look at the end of the text:

    qtlvLKO{...}  -->  picoCTF{...}
  This gives you 7 confirmed letter mappings right away:

  • q → p
  • t → i
  • l → c
  • v → o
  • L → C
  • K → T
  • O → F
  ──────
  ### Step 2: Use Terminal tr to Apply Partial Decryption
  You can use the Linux tr command interactively. As you discover letters, add them to your tr command string.

  Start with what you know:

    cat substution1.txt | tr 'qtlv' 'pico'
  ──────
  ### Step 3: Identify High-Frequency & Short Words
  Look at common English patterns in the partially decrypted text:

  1. Single-letter words:
      • In English, single-letter words are almost always a or I.
      • Text snippet: gej g kyqj → g is a.
  2. The most common 3-letter word (the):
      • The word kwj appears multiple times: ...kwj osgx...
      • kwj → the (giving k → t, w → h, j → e).
  3. Two-letter words:
      • vo (since v = o) → must be of (giving o → f).
      • tb (since t = i) → must be is (giving b → s).
      • gu (since g = a) → an / as → guz is and (u → n, z → d).

  ──────
  ### Step 4: Fill in Words Like a Crossword Puzzle

  Now update your tr command with all discovered letters:

    cat substution1.txt | tr 'qtlvLKObwgkjoeuz' 'picoCTFshatefrnd'

  Looking at the output now:

  • qevmsjr → p r o [m] l e m → clearly problem (m → b)
  • bitssb → s [i] i l l s → clearly skills (i → k)
  • dwtlw → [d] h i c h → clearly which (d → w)
  • OE3AH3ULY in the flag → FR3[A]U3NCY → clearly FR3QU3NCY (A → Q)
  ──────
  ### Step 5: Full Terminal Command

  Once you have your mapping, you can run:

    cat substution1.txt | tr \
      'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ' \
      'qswrvauketcb.f.pmliohgy.dQSWRVAUKETCBPMLIOHGY.D'

  Or quickly in Python:

    cipher = "qtlvLKO{OE3AH3ULY_4774LI5_4E3_L001_6J0659OM}"
    mapping = str.maketrans("qtlvLKOfbwejsoxydtuzrmiha", "picoCTFvshateflgywindmbkQ")
    print(cipher.translate(mapping))

